#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163031} :activity/id #uuid "9c611ad4-705d-5751-a473-84f3e834b6d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049503 :topic/id #uuid "fa18866e-f310-5b54-8bcb-21e9073c9cde" :topic/math-academy-id 3134} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163031 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163306 :tutorial/content "The invertible matrix theorem tells us that if $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix with full rank (i.e., $\\mathrm{rank}(A)=n$), then there exists a unique inverse matrix $A^{-1}$ such that \n\n$$\nAA^{-1}=A^{-1}\\!A=I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ denotes the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nThere are two questions that we can ask:\n\n- Can we extend the notion of an inverse matrix to one that works for $n\\times n$ matrices $A$ where $\\mathrm{rank}(A) < n?$\n\n- Is it possible to generalize the concept of an inverse to non-square matrices?\n\nThe answer to both questions is yes! This generalization of the inverse of $A$ is called the **pseudoinverse** of $A,$ and is denoted $A^+.$ The dimensions of $A^+$ are the same as $A^T.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the ${\\color{red}{3}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}}$ matrix $A$ and its corresponding pseudoinverse, given below:\n\n![](images/87/87581a69d3d76dbf3b0ebca8941c9a82b5b9443b00e86f32e4e9e4a42ef5e18b.svg)\n\n- Computing $A^{+}\\!A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+}\\,A & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the identity matrix $I_{\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n- Computing $A\\!A^{+},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{+} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *almost* the identity matrix $I_{\\color{red}{3}}.$\n\nWe can explain this result using the following properties of the pseudoinverse:\n\n- *If the columns of an $m\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $A^+A = I_{\\color{blue}{n}}.$*\n\n- *If the rows of an ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times n$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $AA^+ = I_{\\color{red}{m}}.$*\n\nIn the example above, the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, but the rows are not.\n\nMoreover, the notion of a pseudoinverse is consistent with inverse matrices. In particular,\n\n*If $A$ is an invertible square matrix, then $A^{+} = A^{-1}.$*\n\nThis lesson will discuss how to compute the pseudoinverse for any matrix, starting with an important special case." :tutorial/id #uuid "6cec9ecf-529c-566f-9895-3fde09f24253" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6074 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a081b9a8-d058-5b79-8d8b-a8471a1f524a" :step/math-academy-id 36962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163047}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163032 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161902 :question/id #uuid "e0a12130-2908-58fd-a8e0-91083fa30e96" :question/math-academy-id "e-9828"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e82e8b16-51a8-59c8-9ed1-51619335de97" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048143}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511514}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "b8379f09-2e11-58eb-bae4-01a9b4bd2b5b" :step/math-academy-id 36963 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163034}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163033 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161903 :question/id #uuid "64e7f84f-da34-5ae2-b2d1-72373a1d2888" :question/math-academy-id "e-9829"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3460bfd6-8b2f-5a52-891a-4c8b2af5b5e8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521527}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Diagonal Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "a1fe2653-29e8-5527-b4fa-083f233b81c0" :step/math-academy-id 36966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163034 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163307 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix\n\n![](images/98/987aa533ca51a9f101ba590b7ade0753983ae219534342aad42054316b2e02ec.svg)\n\nand its singular value decomposition $A=U \\Sigma V ^T,$ where\n\n![](images/69/69e68ac94c6e25b2609f7d20616d7d564a1225abad748c3e4fd94516cb6df72f.svg)\n\nNotice that $\\Sigma$ contains a row of zeros. In these situations, it's possible to simplify the decomposition of $A.$ This simplified decomposition is called the **reduced singular value decomposition** of $A.$\n\nFirst, we note that $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. In addition, the matrix $\\Sigma$ has two non-zero singular values on its main diagonal, so the rank of $A$ is $\\color{blue}r=2.$ \n\nTo find our reduced SVD, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we remove all zero rows and columns from $\\Sigma.$ In our example, we should delete the $3$rd row since it contains only zeros. Notice that we obtain a square invertible matrix of rank ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$ \n![](images/a5/a56440b8808003f25bec4b61d4d06af7274a5eea68da4e94af9e91af8d64d02a.svg)\n\n- Next, we remove all extra columns of $U$ starting from the right, keeping only $\\color{blue}2$ columns. In our example, we should delete the $3$rd column:\n![](images/e4/e4ba93ef3588affc421af821cb9de1051749396c69a9e64523399f7fc4e3bc5b.svg)\n\n- Finally, we remove all extra columns of $V$ starting from the right, keeping only $\\color{blue}2$ columns. In our example, we should keep $V$ as it is since it already has only $2$ columns: \n![](images/b1/b146b37075dda593ded5b86d58e6cfba3de463e01727b39756a6b90cbb88a95f.svg)\n\nThe reduced singular value decomposition of $A$ is then given by\n\n$$\nA = U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T.\n$$\n\nSo, in our example, we have\n\n![](images/c1/c1eed35f9b7aa3214e9783f8f80952e57adca6c4d4b81efaeb49255b5973f3af.svg)\n\nIn general, a singular value decomposition \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\n \n\nof an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is not unique. However, we can always simplify it to the corresponding reduced singular value decomposition \n\n$$\nA=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the following properties:\n\n- $\\Sigma_r$ is a square diagonal matrix of size $r\\times r,$ where $r\\leq \\min\\{m,n\\}$ is the rank of $A.$ The main diagonal of $\\Sigma_r$ contains the non-zero singular values $\\sigma_1, \\sigma_2, \\ldots, \\sigma_r$ of $A$ in descending order.\n\n- $U_r$ is a $m\\times r$ matrix obtained by keeping only the first $r$ columns of $U.$\n\n- $V_r$ is a $n \\times r$ matrix obtained by keeping only the first $r$ columns of $V.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "924b4229-0b49-5d81-9255-b9be0ec07ab6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6075 :tutorial/title "Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "0c540186-c349-53ef-8094-366eb44f526a" :step/math-academy-id 36969 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161904 :question/id #uuid "66c34c76-6243-50b8-832f-027abb6ab736" :question/math-academy-id "e-9830"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff858037-da99-53e9-acdc-e4963e7d765e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511568}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse of a Matrix Given Its Reduced Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "03964208-b7f5-5c5d-ab41-385b66f7efbd" :step/math-academy-id 36970 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163039}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163039 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163309 :tutorial/content "Recall that the least-squares solution of the system \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf x=\\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nis a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that $\\| A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\|$ is minimized. \n\nThe least-squares solution is not unique when $A$ has linearly dependent columns. However, it can be shown that a so-called **optimal least-squares solution** $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}$ always exists. The optimal least-squares solution is the solution to the least squares problem with the shortest possible length.\n\nIt turns out that the optimal least-squares solution can be found using the pseudoinverse of $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x^\\ast} = A^{+} \\mathbf{b}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6f731acb-e452-5433-88a5-1e2c66fd5a84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6078 :tutorial/title "The Optimal Least-Squares Solution"} :step/id #uuid "f03c737f-a30a-5ef1-8ade-d5c1bd49e933" :step/math-academy-id 36979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163313 :tutorial/content "There are many ways to find the pseudoinverse of a given matrix $A.$ One way is to use the singular value decomposition of $A{:}$\n\nSpecifically, if \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V ^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA^+ = V \\, \\Sigma^+ \\, U^T,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\Sigma^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bd110f79-cfe0-58f8-b7e6-ea9baa7814d9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6102 :tutorial/title "Finding the Pseudoinverse Using Singular Value Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "cef3aa4e-1b1d-50ea-b285-5cc2dcac6f73" :step/math-academy-id 37091 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163032}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163314 :tutorial/content "For an *almost* zero ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal only, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the *almost* zero ${\\color{blue}{n}}\\times {\\color{red}{m}}$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal only.\n\nFor example, for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/d6/d6d6a167747f99c5a48be59ae3e11ca7b5a9188e80ddeb63b8c0f193d991b18c.svg)\n\nwe calculate its pseudoinverse as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Replace the non-zero elements on the main diagonal with their multiplicative inverses.\n\n- **Step 2:** Transpose the resulting matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1/3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8709222e-61fc-512e-82ff-3564c1513100" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6103 :tutorial/title "The Pseudoinverse of an Almost Diagonal Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e5289df4-d145-54b7-a6f3-e8e13a522ee7" :step/math-academy-id 37092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163033}}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition and the Pseudoinverse Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162228}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420796} :activity/id #uuid "6fcc24e3-a2d7-5c60-8e6f-28eb1ce8232f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049503 :topic/id #uuid "fa18866e-f310-5b54-8bcb-21e9073c9cde" :topic/math-academy-id 3134} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c5eab7-61e5-58ba-983c-a7f55e21301d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420765} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1203aa-43a1-5ff4-889a-1439cc8945c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420767} {:answer/id #uuid "73f62c7b-39b6-567c-8e60-5bf5c983d0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420772} {:answer/id #uuid "7e01a274-8738-5cf2-a673-1055c4c67b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420775} {:answer/id #uuid "fb51fb6a-1738-5259-951f-347a4793bbda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420767} :answer-field/id #uuid "a01449e0-fee7-54b8-bc93-a094ffce75c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a18b0561-3c55-5481-a95c-144e99d62fb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-138620" :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{10}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{V}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ed2be1f-10a4-55eb-be70-17513ed55a84" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420797}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0755beb3-73a6-5399-8b09-ddef07ddf09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420759} {:answer/id #uuid "32b39ef8-9dd6-57b9-ab9a-17113d1beb75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420764} {:answer/id #uuid "579ce615-12df-5c19-81bd-6fbd6731105e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420770} {:answer/id #uuid "890f27c1-0c91-5b46-9f4e-6f30606040c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420776} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e09d3c-fdfb-5162-bbc7-9d78cc139800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420776} :answer-field/id #uuid "22532903-9de5-5ea6-88dc-56a50c21802f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "237a5725-5a0e-5e90-89ea-d6700e3a977b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138563" :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "953d8034-af91-529e-ac10-86829389807d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420798}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420793 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d71454-0458-5173-92e2-d24ae7d33b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420761} {:answer/id #uuid "299c3fa6-9fa8-5565-8634-09054fedc6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420762} {:answer/id #uuid "48db3d3c-30c7-55ab-a903-b5f38c30500f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420768} {:answer/id #uuid "96fb9c14-df10-5b90-9b22-46eaa98a547f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420777} {:answer/id #uuid "f34f3108-25b4-52ad-8686-5c2f57c96aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420782}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420777} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce23d7e-7b89-51d8-91ae-2e6f5b73477c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7a5359f-8967-570a-b634-daaee8dbc9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-138535" :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{10} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "960994c9-02d9-50a9-91bf-ae3a30818221" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e553566-a4fb-554e-8652-c4ff524a08ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420763} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcafdf4-daf9-5fb3-9853-a3d9f99769a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420769} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba8d035-4562-532b-b4de-8633397e74bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420774} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6598fd-20cd-597a-8708-3c1fffbbe65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420778} {:answer/id #uuid "efe97a60-f805-5797-98cb-412041423fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420781}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420763} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d63e1b9-0a9f-5b44-a041-bc2d944d4dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f2315875-6b0f-5492-8e9e-75741c68b770" :question/math-academy-id "q-138658" :question/problem "$$\n{U}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{V}_{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A={U}_{r}{Σ}_{r}{V}_{r}^{T}$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}_{r}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of ${Σ}_{r}.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n{V}_{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{U}_{r}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9c983558-d20b-57a2-8b1f-9e21e82a0e92"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1243f8e9-7cde-50e1-